  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNFPA Women and Girls First, Phase II, 2020-2023</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">UNFPA Women and Girls First, fas II, 2020-2023</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Women and Girls First Programme is a joint donor programme managed by UNFPA. The Programme is set up to work for empowerment of the most vulnerable women and girls through pioneering integrated sexual and reproductive health and rights, mental health and psychosocial support as well as gender-based violence response and prevention service. 

The Women and Girls First Programme Phase II aims to continuing the work done during the first phase of the programme. The continued focus is to work for people who are furthest behind in conflict affected states, for women, girls and young people to realise their sexual and reproductive health and rights and fulfill their potential through improved gender equality and protection from gender-based violence as well as access to mental health and psycho social support services.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Women and Girls First Programmet &#xe4;r ett givargemensamt program som drivs av UNFPA. Programmets design &#xe4;r unikt och integrerar arbete med sexuell och reproduktiv h&#xe4;lsa och r&#xe4;ttigheter, mental h&#xe4;lsa samt arbete mot k&#xf6;nsbaserat v&#xe5;ld i Myanmars konfliktdrabbade omr&#xe5;den. 

Programmet syftar till att verka f&#xf6;r att de mest utsatta m&#xe4;nniskorna i konfliktdrabbade omr&#xe5;den, framf&#xf6;rallt kvinnor, flickor och unga kan &#xe5;tnjuta sina r&#xe4;ttigheter genom f&#xf6;rb&#xe4;ttrat system och service f&#xf6;r skydd och tillg&#xe5;ng till sexuell och reproduktiv h&#xe4;lsa och r&#xe4;ttigheter, mental h&#xe4;lsa och psykosocialt st&#xf6;d. Fas II av Women and Girls First-programmet bygger vidare p&#xe5; arbetet som p&#xe5;b&#xf6;rjades under den f&#xf6;rsta fasen av programmet.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women, girls and young people [1] who are the furthest behind [2] in conflict affected states realise their sexual and reproductive health and rights and fulfill their potential through improved gender equality and protection from gender-based violence (GBV) as well as access to mental health and psychosocial support services.

[1] While there are no universally accepted definitions of adolescence and youth, the United Nations understands adolescents to include persons aged 10-19 years and youth as those between 15-24
years for statistical purposes without prejudice to other definitions by Member States. Together, adolescents and youth are referred to as young people, encompassing the ages of 10-24
years.
[2] including IDPs, conflict affected people, GBV survivors, LGBTQIA, People Living with HIV, YKAP (young key affected populations), elderly and people with disabilities.

The programme is aimed with the following objectives:
1) Systems are responsive to the needs of women, girls and young people regarding sexual reproductive health and rights (SRHR), the right to safety from violence and safety in emergencies, are adopted and implemented,
2) Improved access to a quality, comprehensive, rights-based package of integrated SRHR, GBV and Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS) services
3) Enhanced community resilience and social cohesion towards realisation and and localisation of SRHR, GBV and MHPSS outcomes</narrative>
  </description>
